 "paragraphs": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p001.s01",
     "source": "They are, though not absolutely malevolent, believed to be a peevish, repining, and envious race, who enjoy, in the subterranean recesses, a kind of shadowy splendour."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s02",
     "source": "The Highlanders are at all times unwilling to speak of them, but especially on Friday, when their influence is supposed to be particularly extensive."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s03",
     "source": "As they are supposed to be invisibly present, they are at all times to be spoken of with respect."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s04",
     "source": "The fairies of Scotland are represented as a diminutive race of beings, of a mixed or rather dubious nature, capricious in their dispositions, and mischievous in their resentment."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s05",
     "source": "They inhabit the interior of green hills, chiefly those of a conical form, in Gaelic termed Sighan , on which they lead their dances by moonlight, impressing upon the surface the marks of circles, which sometimes appear yellow and blasted, sometimes of a deep green hue, and within which it is dangerous to sleep, or to be found after sunset."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s06",
     "source": "The removal of those large portions of turf, which thunderbolts sometimes scoop out of the ground with singular regularity, is also ascribed to their agency."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s07",
     "source": "Cattle which are suddenly seized with the cramp, or some similar disorder, are said to be elf-shot, and the approved cure is to chafe the parts affected with a blue bonnet, which, it may be readily believed, often restores the circulation."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s08",
     "source": "The triangular flints frequently found in Scotland, with which the ancient inhabitants probably barbed their shafts, are supposed to be the weapons of fairy resentment, and are termed elf arrowheads."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s09",
     "source": "The rude brazen battle-axes of the ancients, commonly called “celts,” are also ascribed to their manufacture."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s10",
     "source": "But, like the Gothic duergar, their skill is not confined to the fabrication of arms; for they are heard sedulously hammering in linns, precipices, and rocky or cavernous situations, where, like the dwarfs of the mines mentioned by George Agricola, they busy themselves in imitating the actions and the various employments of men."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s11",
     "source": "The Brook of Beaumont, for example, which passes in its course by numerous linns and caverns, is notorious for being haunted by the fairies; and the perforated and rounded stones which are formed by trituration in its channels are termed by the vulgar fairy cups and dishes."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s12",
     "source": "A beautiful reason is assigned by Fletcher for the fays frequenting streams and fountains."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s13",
     "source": "He tells us of"
    }
   ],
